{"type":"text","timestamp":1792167038330,"sessionID":"ses_eba855e44ffeHYr7R0IW8cEN0E","part":{"id":"prt_1457acd6d001MZPS62Nms2uJPU","messageID":"msg_1457ab300001FXdfmppSB7edxS","sessionID":"ses_eba855e44ffeHYr7R0IW8cEN0E","type":"text","text":"VERDICT: BLOCK\n\n## Blocking\n- tally/__main__.py:17 — The fix only catches `FileNotFoundError`, but the issue says to \"exit 1 if any file could not be read\". So `python -m tally somedir good.txt` (`IsADirectoryError`), an unreadable file (`PermissionError`), or a non-UTF-8 file (`UnicodeDecodeError`) still crashes with a traceback and never prints the counts for `good.txt`. Catch `OSError` and use `e.strerror` in the message, and decide how decode errors should be handled.\n- tests/test_main.py:17 — The new code isn't tested for the other read failures above (a directory or a file with no read permission). The behaviour the issue asks for (\"could not be read\" → keep going, exit 1) has no tests beyond the missing-file case.\n\n## Non-blocking\n- tests/test_main.py:22 — The test uses the relative path `\"nope.txt\"`, so it depends on the working directory. If a `nope.txt` exists where the tests run, the test fails. Use a missing path inside the temp dir `d` instead.\n- tally/__main__.py:18 — The message text \"No such file or directory\" is hardcoded. Using `e.strerror` would give the right text for every kind of error.\n- tests/test_main.py:27 — There's no test for the case where every file is missing (exit 1, nothing on stdout) or where several files are missing (one stderr line each).","time":{"start":1792167038317,"end":1792167038328}}}
